Ladybird keeps the enclosing block in the containing-block chain of
an absolutely positioned descendant of a positioned inline. Painting
and hit-testing inherit the clips at that inline, so an overflow clip
outside the inline clips the descendant. A position: relative inline
doesn’t contain fixed-position descendants, though.

Blink locates the positioned inline ancestor with LayoutObject::
ContainerForAbsolutePosition(), then resolves its enclosing block in
ContainingBlockForAbsolutePosition() via InclusiveContainingBlock().
Its paint property builder, FragmentPaintPropertyTreeBuilder, saves
that inline’s current context in UpdateOutOfFlowContext() whenever
CanContainAbsolutePositionObjects() is true. So, its absolute children
inherit the inline’s clip chain, matching Ladybird’s behavior.

WebKit’s RenderObject::containingBlockForPositionType() finds the
ancestor that can contain absolutely positioned objects, then uses
its enclosing block. RenderLayer::calculateClipRects() gives an
in-flow positioned renderer’s overflow clip to its abspos descendants.
So, its positioned inlines preserve the clipping ancestor’s clip too.

Gecko makes a positioned inline an absolute containing block in
nsCSSFrameConstructor::ConstructInline(). Its display-list builder
records the containing block’s clip chain for out-of-flow descendants
in nsDisplayListBuilder::MarkFramesForDisplayList(), via the clip
state’s GetClipChainForContainingBlockDescendants(). The containing
inline therefore preserves its ancestor’s overflow clip, which is the
same clipping behavior Ladybird applies.
